WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:09.690
My name is Derrick Winje.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.570
I am the director of sneaker operations

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:13.830
at The Cobblers in Miami, Florida.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.650
Today, we're gonna be sole-swapping

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:19.210
a original 1985 pair of Jordan Chicago 1s

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:20.750
with a donor sole.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.630
And these actually released in 1985,

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:25.210
with a retail of just $65.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.670
For reference, the same
pair in 1985 for $65

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.840
is now in the aftermarket, brand new,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.160
for about $20,000,

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.085
and that's if you can even find a pair.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:38.980
The first step that we need to do

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:40.321
is acquire a donor shoe.

00:00:40.321 --> 00:00:42.920
In this case, the client
had provided a donor shoe,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:44.780
which is actually the 2015 Retro,

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.870
which is merely just a
rerelease of the shoe.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.747
On this particular pair,
the customer requested

00:00:49.747 --> 00:00:51.240
that we leave the upper of the shoe,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.730
which is all of the leather.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:53.790
He did not want it cleaned.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:54.830
He did not want it painted.

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.920
He didn't want any conditioners.

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:58.710
So we need to carefully take off the sole

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.701
without, you know, damaging the sole

00:01:00.701 --> 00:01:01.770
or altering anything.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.040
In this case, the upper of the donor shoe

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:04.873
is not as important,

00:01:04.873 --> 00:01:06.810
but sometimes we use those
parts for other things.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.490
So, what I like to start with

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.290
is just using heat and acetone.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.900
Ideally, I'll pour the
acetone inside of the shoe

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.500
to actually loosen up the glue

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.040
that is on the footbed and
the sidewalls of the shoe

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.080
to kind of break that down

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.620
to give me an entry point in the side.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.378
Once we're able to get to that layer

00:01:22.378 --> 00:01:23.720
of stitching on the sidewalls,

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:25.520
we can then use an X-Acto knife,

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.580
as you see I'm using here,

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.920
or you can also use a seam
ripper to undo those stitches.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.450
Once the sidewall and stitching is undone,

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:33.860
it should be pretty easy from there.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.500
We can either just pull the upper apart

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.560
or continue using heat or the acetone

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.830
to break up that glue

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.139
to separate the sole from the upper here.

00:01:46.975 --> 00:01:47.980
It's a similar process

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:50.830
to remove the original
upper from the sole,

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:52.300
if it's not already detached.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.633
In this case, there is stitching here,

00:01:53.633 --> 00:01:55.032
as you can see.

00:01:55.032 --> 00:01:57.700
However, we don't want to use excess heat,

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:58.868
and we also definitely don't want to

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:00.243
pour acetone into the shoe,

00:02:00.243 --> 00:02:02.730
as that would damage the
upper of the original.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:03.563
On the sides here,

00:02:03.563 --> 00:02:05.310
you'll see that I use a bone folder,

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.418
which is just bone from an animal.

00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:09.595
It's just a -- not a sharp edge,

00:02:09.595 --> 00:02:12.130
but a blunt enough edge that
I can open up the sidewalls

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:14.272
to get down into that stitching there.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.210
Once the two pieces are
completely separated,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.237
we're going to remove all of the dust

00:02:26.237 --> 00:02:28.460
and crumbling inside of the original shoe,

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:29.560
as well as from the upper,

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.800
to prepare this upper of the original shoe

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.541
to be put on the donor shoe.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.600
We use a contact cement.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:46.660
It is made for shoes.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.040
It is flexible. It's clear.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.190
So it's perfect for what we're doing here.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:53.548
And it's very important

00:02:53.548 --> 00:02:55.060
with any type of restoration we do,

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.026
when we're working with glue,

00:02:56.026 --> 00:02:57.476
especially in this case,

00:02:57.476 --> 00:02:59.100
that we get a clean surface

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.270
and get that fully prepped

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.500
and all of the old glue,

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.540
any dust or any buildup, oil,

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.770
water that may have seeped into that shoe.

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:06.980
The reasoning for this is because

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:08.163
if we don't have a clean surface

00:03:08.163 --> 00:03:09.290
for the glue to adhere to,

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.120
the glue is not gonna fully do its job

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:12.290
in holding that shoe together

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.510
for the longest period of time.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.360
We will apply one thin layer
to the upper, in this case,

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.262
as well as on the sole of the donor shoe,

00:03:20.262 --> 00:03:22.189
and we allow that to dry.

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:26.940
A tip that I like to use

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.570
is actually to let this glue overcure,

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:30.980
so it gives me more
time to fit the sneaker,

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.920
as opposed to having to put
it all together in one shot.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.761
And this, again, is so we can line up

00:03:35.761 --> 00:03:37.400
the upper properly on the sole,

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.993
so there's no distinction
between the original sole

00:03:39.993 --> 00:03:42.752
and the donor sole being
lined up on this 1985.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:51.900
Once we have heated up the glue

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:53.070
and pressed it with our hand

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:55.150
to make sure everything
is fitting properly,

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:56.480
we will then just double-check

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.280
and make sure everything is
lined up the way we like,

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.114
so everything looks original.

00:04:02.705 --> 00:04:05.310
We'll then take the
original last from the shoe,

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.460
which is just the interior of the shoe,

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.450
if you can think of it
as what shoemakers use

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.140
to make and shape shoes.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.381
We'll place that into the shoe,

00:04:12.381 --> 00:04:15.560
and then we will take it
over to the bladder press.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:16.840
What the bladder press is gonna do

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.760
is basically give us that extra security

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:20.240
and push the shoe together

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.000
and make sure that the
glue is fully bonded

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.940
and has a strong hold to it.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.110
Once we're done, you can see here

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:26.810
that we actually stitch by hand.

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:27.670
There is a machine,

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:28.920
but with something this delicate

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.460
and this fragile and rare,

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:32.960
we actually prefer stitching it by hand,

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:34.130
just so we know that every stitch

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:36.290
is fit perfectly into the original hole

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:39.100
to make things look as
original as possible.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.308
So, you can see, this isn't a

00:04:40.308 --> 00:04:41.790
pure white thread we're using.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:42.990
We actually have an aged

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.098
or more of a vanilla cream thread,

00:04:45.098 --> 00:04:47.310
which is gonna match the
original look of that shoe.

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.300
If we were to put a bright,

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.900
brilliant white lace or thread in there,

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:52.750
it would kind of take
away from the originality

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.530
of keeping the upper a little distressed

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:56.110
and having the sole looking brand new

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:58.563
on top of an old, distressed 1985 shoe.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.580
We actually add a little bit of wax here,

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:04.747
to imitate the original shoe upon release.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:11.330
Now that everything's stitched up,

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:13.680
it may not be mind-blowing
before and after

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.580
just putting a sole onto a new shoe,

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.090
but to us, in these little details,

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.210
this makes the shoe complete,

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:19.225
and I think the customer is gonna be

00:05:19.225 --> 00:05:21.217
very happy with these results.

